law.body:
The site and this notice are subject to Gabonese law, without depriving members of the mandatory protections of the country where they live. The main applicable texts are:

– Ordinance No. 0011/PR/2026 of 26 February 2026 regulating the use of social networks through digital platforms, ratified by Law No. 003/2026 of 5 October 2026;
– Law No. 019/2016 of 9 August 2016 establishing the Communication Code, as amended by Ordinance No. 0012/PR/2026;
– Law No. 001/2011 of 25 September 2011 on the protection of personal data, as amended by Law No. 025/2023 of 12 July 2023;
– Law No. 025/2021 of 28 December 2021 on electronic transactions;
– Law No. 027/2023 of 12 July 2023 regulating cybersecurity and the fight against cybercrime;
– for members who live in the European Union, Regulation (EU) 2016/679 on data protection (GDPR).

neverCollected.body:
– no consular data: no passport, no personal identification number, no civil status record, no case file. The consular registration attestation, when it opens, will be limited to a yes or a no, a country, a post and a date;
– on entering the network, no date of birth, no postal address, no identity document;
– no sensitive data within the meaning of the Law (health, religion, opinions, ethnic origin, sex life). If you publish such data yourself, it becomes visible to the audience you have chosen;
– no geographic position: the location of your device is never requested;
– no audience measurement, no advertising tracker, no third-party script.

purposes.body:
Each processing operation has a purpose and a basis provided for by law (Law No. 001/2011 as amended, Article 71):

– creating your account, signing you in and checking that you can enter the network — basis: performance of the Terms of use;
– suggesting, when you enter, a display name and a handle based on your identite.ga identity, which you can change — basis: performance of the Terms of use; this data is kept for 24 hours at most;
– showing your profile to members, listing you in the directory, sending you notifications, requesting a consular attestation — basis: your consent, given purpose by purpose;
– running the feed, connections, messaging and pages — basis: performance of the Terms of use;
– receiving and handling reports, applying the rules, meeting legal time limits, keeping reported content, responding to authorities — basis: the publisher’s legal obligations;
– protecting the network against abuse, fraud and intrusion, and keeping a record of administration decisions — basis: the legitimate interest of the publisher and the members.

Your data is not used for advertising, for resale or to build a commercial profile.

recipients.body:
Apart from members, according to your choices, your data is passed on only to the providers that make the service work. They are bound to the publisher by contract and act only on its instructions (Law No. 001/2011 as amended, Articles 115 to 117):

– Convex, Inc. (United States of America): the network’s database, files and server functions;
– Vercel Inc. (United States of America): delivery of the web application; it processes your IP address and technical browsing data;
– Resend, Inc. (United States of America): sending of sign-in codes by e-mail; it receives your e-mail address and the code, never your posts or your messages;
– identite.ga, a digital identity service published by {{publisherName}}: when you choose that sign-in;
– your browser’s notification services (Apple, Google, Mozilla, Microsoft): if you turn on device notifications; they receive a signal with no content;
– consulat.ga, when the consular attestation opens and if you consent: it will receive your digital identity identifier or a fingerprint of your e-mail address, and will reply with a yes or a no, a country, a post and dates.

Calls and live broadcasts are not available in DIASPORA. If they become so, their provider will be added to this list before they open.

Your data may be passed to the judicial and administrative authorities that request it in the forms provided for by law. It is neither sold, nor rented, nor transferred.

retention.body:
Your data is kept for as long as your account exists, then erased. In detail:

– account, profile, connections, posts, comments and messages: until you remove them, or until your account is erased;
– sign-in codes: 5 minutes; session: 7 days, renewed as long as you use the service;
– statuses: 24 hours;
– name and handles suggested on entry from identite.ga: 24 hours at most, erased as soon as your profile is created;
– notifications: 180 days after they are read, 365 days if they have not been read;
– supporting documents for a page claim: deleted as soon as the request is withdrawn, or 90 days after the decision;
– files rejected by the antivirus scan: 90 days;
– export of your data: the file is assembled in your browser; the record of the request is kept for 24 hours;
– reports, moderation decisions and the administration log: kept to justify the decisions; when your account is erased, they are detached from you as a person.

Account erasure: nothing is erased during the 30 days that follow your request, and you can cancel it. After that period, your sessions are closed and your data is erased, including the messages you have sent. Only a record without a name shows that the erasure took place.

Longer retention takes place only if a law requires it of the publisher or if proceedings are under way: reported content, an authority’s requisition, a dispute. It is then limited to the data concerned and to the time needed.

security.body:
The publisher puts in place technical and organisational measures suited to the risks (Law No. 001/2011 as amended, Article 113):

– exchanges between your device and the service are encrypted in transit;
– sign-in is passwordless, by one-time code or by digital identity, and the number of attempts is limited;
– every read and every write is checked by the server: membership of the network, the person’s rights, rate;
– a refusal of access never says whether the content exists, whether it is private or whether its author has blocked you;
– uploaded files go through an antivirus scan before being served;
– notifications sent outside the application carry no content;
– administration and moderation decisions are logged;
– no consular data enters the network.

Messages are not end-to-end encrypted. As no system is infallible, protect access to your inbox and close your sessions on a device you no longer control, using “Sign out everywhere”.

rights.body:
Law No. 001/2011 as amended gives you the following rights:

– to be informed of what is done with your data (Article 98): that is the purpose of this page;
– to access your data and obtain a copy of it (Articles 43 to 47);
– to have it rectified or completed (Articles 50 to 52);
– to have it erased (Article 53);
– to have its processing restricted (Articles 55 to 57);
– to receive it in a reusable format, or to have it transmitted to another service (Articles 58 and 59);
– to object to processing, and in every case to direct marketing (Articles 60 to 65);
– not to be subject to a decision based solely on automated processing (Articles 66 to 69);
– to withdraw a consent at any time (Article 73);
– to lodge a complaint with the APDPVP.

Ordinance No. 0011/PR/2026 also gives every user of a social network the right to have their data rectified, completed, updated or erased, and to retrieve it (Articles 12 to 15).

If you live in the European Union, the GDPR gives you equivalent rights (Articles 15 to 22).

exercise.body:
Most of your rights can be exercised directly in the service, without asking anyone:

– changing your profile and setting the visibility of each item of information: in your profile and your settings;
– withdrawing a consent: in your settings;
– exporting all your data to a file: in “My data”;
– requesting the erasure of your account, or only leaving DIASPORA: in “My data”;
– viewing the moderation decisions that concern you and appealing against them: in “My data”.

“My data” stays open without membership, after a departure or during a suspension.

For any other request, write to {{publisherEmail}} from your account’s e-mail address, or by post to {{publisherName}}, {{publisherAddress}}. The reply is free of charge. It reaches you within one month of receipt of the request; this period may be extended by two months for a complex request, and you are then informed within the first month (Articles 93 and 95).

cookies.body:
DIASPORA uses no advertising cookie and no audience-measurement tracker. It records on your device only what is strictly necessary for the service you request; the Law does not require prior consent in that case (Law No. 001/2011 as amended, Article 104).

– session cookies: they keep you signed in and end when you sign out;
– a language cookie, kept for one year;
– in the browser’s storage: your appearance (theme, light or dark mode), your post drafts, your latest choices of country and pages, the information cards you have closed and, if you turn on notifications, a randomly generated device identifier;
– a page that can be viewed offline, kept by the browser.

When you sign out, session data and drafts are erased from the device; the appearance is kept. You can erase everything from your browser settings.

complaint.body:
If you believe your rights are not being respected, write to the publisher first. You can also refer the matter, at any time, to:

– the Authority for the Protection of Personal Data and Privacy (APDPVP), Haut de Gué-Gué, en face de l'entrée de service de l'hôtel Radisson Blu, Libreville — telephone +241 01 44 31 34 — contact@apdpvp.ga — apdpvp.ga;
– if you live in the European Union, the data protection authority of your country of residence: in France, the Commission nationale de l'informatique et des libertés (CNIL).

purpose.body:
These terms set the rules for access to and use of DIASPORA, the social network of Gabonese citizens living abroad, published by {{publisherName}} (see the Legal notice). Together with the Community rules, they form the contract between each member and the publisher; the Privacy page describes the processing of personal data.

DIASPORA allows its members:

– to keep a profile and choose who sees each item of information on it;
– to post, comment and react in the network’s feed and in the feed of their country of residence;
– to connect with other members and write to them through messaging;
– to follow and run the pages of diaspora associations and companies.

Other spaces (groups and events, services, jobs, mutual aid, mentoring, arrival kit) open step by step, on the decision of the network’s administration. These terms apply to them from the moment they open.

access.body:
DIASPORA is a closed network. To enter it, you must:

– be a Gabonese citizen;
– live outside Gabon, in a country where the network is open; the list of open countries is given at the entrance;
– be an adult, that is, be 18 years old or over.

The network is not open to minors. The Law sets the digital age of majority at 16 and prohibits the creation of any account before that age (Ordinance No. 0011/PR/2026, Articles 16 and 17); it also makes the processing of the data of a person under 18 subject to the consent of their parents (Law No. 001/2011 as amended, Article 74). By entering, you declare that you are an adult. An account opened by a minor is closed and its data is erased.

Your membership has a status, calculated by the service from the evidence provided:

– pending: you can read the network but not post in it, as long as no evidence is provided;
– declared: a verified member has sponsored you, or you have signed in with your identite.ga digital identity;
– verified: your digital identity has a sufficient assurance level and a verified member has sponsored you, or you have been admitted among the founding members. The consular registration attestation will also give this status when that service opens.

Sponsoring a person means attesting that you know them and that they meet these conditions. A sponsorship can be withdrawn. The badges shown on a profile are calculated by the service: none can be bought or declared.

The publisher may refuse or withdraw a membership that does not meet these conditions.

conduct.body:
You use the network lawfully and fairly. The Community rules say what is allowed and what is not; they form part of these terms.

In particular, it is prohibited:

– to publish, share or relay content that is illegal, hateful, violent, sexual or misleading;
– to harass, threaten or intimidate a person;
– to pass yourself off as another person, an organisation or an institution;
– to publish another person’s personal data without their consent;
– to collect members’ data, to use a robot or any other automated means, or to bypass a technical limit of the service;
– to attempt to access an account, data or a part of the service that is not open to you;
– to solicit members without having been invited to, or to send them bulk messages;
– to bypass a moderation measure, for example by opening another account.

You are responsible for what you publish, share or relay. Ordinance No. 0011/PR/2026 makes the person who produces, distributes or amplifies illegal content liable.

liability.body:
Each member is responsible for their content, their exchanges and the use of their account. The managers of a page are responsible for what is published on it.

The publisher is responsible for the operation of the service and for the diligence of its moderation. It promptly removes illegal content as soon as it becomes aware of it and meets the time limits the Law sets for it.

Within the limits the Law allows, the publisher is not liable for:

– agreements concluded between members or their consequences;
– the content of the sites to which a member links;
– an interruption of the service caused by a third party or by force majeure;
– damage arising from the failure to observe these terms by the member who complains of it.

Nothing in these terms excludes a liability that the law prohibits excluding, nor the rights that the law of your country of residence gives you as a consumer.

